  "writeup_markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Asháninka\"\nsubtitle: \"Peru\"\nregion: \"Latin America\"\ntags: [ethnography, latin-america]\n---\n\n## At a glance\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Who | The Asháninka, an Indigenous people of the Amazonian rainforests |\n| Where | Central-eastern Peru (Junín, Pasco, Huánuco, Ucayali) and Brazilian state of Acre |\n| How many | Between 25,000 and 100,000 people |\n| Language | Asháninka, an Arawakan language |\n| Religion | No organized religious system documented; resisted Catholic missions |\n| Known for | Woven cotton robes (cushma) made entirely by women · Face painting with red annatto seeds · River-based settlements in the Amazon · Biodiversity-based subsistence living · Language preservation efforts |\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Asháninka are Indigenous Amazonians who live in river valleys across central-eastern Peru and Brazil. They are known worldwide for their handwoven cotton robes and distinctive red face painting. Their way of life centers on slash-and-burn farming, hunting, fishing, and forest gathering, treating biodiversity as essential to survival.\n\n## Material culture\n\nAsháninka material culture is made entirely from natural materials—cotton textiles, forest seeds, animal bones and teeth, feathers, and cane—with no pottery or metalwork in the record.\n\n### Textile & pattern traditions\n\nCotton weaving is the heart of Asháninka material culture, with all cloth made and dyed by women on household looms.\n\n- **Cushma** — The central Asháninka garment—a full-length cotton robe dyed brown or bright royal blue, ornamented with seeds at the shoulders, taking up to three months for a woman to complete.\n- **Striped woven cloth** — Vertical stripes in dark and light brown or beige appear throughout Asháninka woven cotton, used for tunics, bags, and hoods.\n- **Painted cloth** — Decoration applied by hand paint to woven cotton, used on some smocks and bags alongside woven designs.\n- **Baby-carriers** — Woven cotton carriers hung with ornaments of incised bone, seeds, and nuts as a permanent feature.\n\n### Clothing & dress\n\nThe cushma serves as the everyday garment for all Asháninka, with gender distinctions in hair style and ceremonial dress.\n\n- **Cushma** — Cotton robe worn daily by men and women as the standard garment.\n- **Ceremonial head-dress (men)** — Woven circles of palm leaves decorated with feathers, worn by men for ceremonies.\n- **Ceremonial head-dress (women)** — Woven cotton hood or head-cover, often with parrot feathers along the bottom, worn by women for ceremonies.\n- **Face painting** (*achiote or annatto*) — Crushed red seeds of the annatto fruit applied to the face in various designs by men, women, and children.\n- **Feather ornaments** — Belt fringes and hood decorations made from parrot feathers combined with seeds.\n\n### Architecture\n\nNo detailed architectural information is recorded in available sources.\n\n### Ceramics, metalwork & everyday objects\n\nNo pottery or metalwork is documented; everyday objects are subsistence gear made from textiles and natural materials.\n\n- **Hunting and fishing equipment** — Bows, arrows, and spears are the primary tools for hunting and fishing.\n- **Woven cotton bags** — Plain or painted bags used to carry goods, some with sewn and tasselled straps.\n- **Cane rings** — Ring-shaped pieces of worked cane, possibly used as head-dresses.\n\n### Jewelry & body adornment\n\nAsháninka adornment draws entirely from the forest—seeds, animal teeth, and feathers—with no use of metal.\n\n- **Seed necklaces** — Multiple strings of black or colored seeds tied together and fastened around the neck.\n- **Tooth ornaments** — Necklaces and bracelets made from the teeth of tapir, peccary, and monkeys.\n- **Feather adornments** — Brightly colored feathers, especially parrot feathers, worked into necklaces, bracelets, and belt fringes.\n- **Face painting** (*achiote*) — Crushed red seeds of the annatto fruit applied in various designs to the face.\n\n## Music & performance\n\nNo instruments, song genres, or performance occasions are documented in available sources.\n\n## Dance & theatre\n\nNo dances or dramatic forms are documented in available sources.\n\n## Festivals & rituals\n\nNo ceremonial calendar or lifecycle rites are recorded; ceremonies are marked primarily through special dress.\n\n- **Ceremonial dress** — Men wear woven circles of palm leaves decorated with feathers; women wear woven cotton head-dresses for ceremonies.\n- **Face painting** (*achiote*) — Crushed annatto seed applied by all ages in various designs as a general practice, not tied to specific occasions.\n\n## Foodways\n\nAsháninka subsistence is built on slash-and-burn agriculture combined with hunting, fishing, and forest gathering.\n\n- **Cultivated crops** — Yucca roots, sweet potato, corn, bananas, rice, coffee, cacao, and sugar cane are grown in biodiversity-friendly cleared fields.\n- **Hunting and fishing** — Bows, arrows, and spears are used to catch wild game and fish to supplement crops.\n- **Forest gathering** — Fruit and vegetables are gathered from the jungle as a regular part of the diet.\n- **Biodiversity as natural capital** — The forest itself is treated as the foundation of the Asháninka way of life, making forest loss a direct threat to food security.\n\n## Oral tradition & literature\n\nNo folktales or storytelling traditions are recorded; language remains vibrant despite low literacy rates.\n\n- **Language literacy** — Between 10% and 30% of Asháninka speakers read and write their own language; 15% to 25% are literate in Spanish as a second language.\n- **Language standardization efforts** — Attempts to unify closely related Asháninka varieties around a single written standard have not succeeded.\n- **Spanish language programs** — Since 1988, Peru has run programs teaching Spanish to Indigenous peoples including the Asháninka.\n- **Indigenous activism** — Figures like Luzmila Chiricente Mahuanca are known as activists defending Indigenous rights and territories.\n\n## Language & religion\n\nAsháninka belongs to the Arawakan family and is classified as vulnerable; no organized religion is documented, though Catholic missions were historically resisted.\n\n- **Asháninka language** (*Asháninka or Campa*) — An Arawakan language spoken by about 25,000 people in Junín, Pasco, Huánuco, Ucayali and adjacent regions; classed as vulnerable due to forest threats.\n- **Arawakan relatives** — Related languages include Gran Pajonal Campa, Ashéninka, Machiguenga, Matsiguenga, Yine, and others in the Arawakan family.\n- **Offensive term** (*Campa*) — The alternative name Campa, derived from Quechua for 'ragged and dirty,' is offensive to Asháninka speakers.\n- **Catholic mission resistance** — Catholic missions from the 17th to 19th centuries, especially near Cerro de la Sal and Gran Pajonal, were resisted with some success.\n- **Asháninka as official language** — Asháninka holds official language status in the regions where it is spoken.\n\n## Glossary\n\n- *achiote* — Annatto tree; its crushed red seeds used for face painting\n- *annatto* — Bixa orellana; tree whose red seeds are crushed for face paint and dye\n- *Asháninka* — The Indigenous people and their language of central-eastern Peruvian Amazon\n- *Campa* — An alternative name for Asháninka (now considered offensive)\n- *Arawakan* — Maipurean language family to which Asháninka belongs\n- *Gran Pajonal* — Interfluvial grassland region where Asháninka settlements are located\n- *Cerro de la Sal* — Salt Mountain; location where Asháninka resisted Catholic missions\n- *slash-and-burn* — Agricultural method of clearing forest land by burning for cultivation\n- *Junín* — Peruvian region where Asháninka people live\n- *Pasco* — Peruvian region where Asháninka people live\n- *Huánuco* — Peruvian region where Asháninka people live\n- *Ucayali* — Peruvian region where Asháninka people live\n- *Ene* — River in Asháninka territory\n\n## Sources & further reading\n\n- Stefano Varese, *Salt of the Mountain: Campa Asháninka History and Resistance in the Peruvian Jungle*, University of Oklahoma Press, 2002 (ISBN 978-0-8061-3446-8)\n- Rubén Cushimariano Romano and Richer C. Sebastián Q., *Diccionario asháninka–castellano*, preliminary version, 2009\n- Mily Crevels, \"Language Endangerment in South America: The Clock Is Ticking\", in Lyle Campbell and Veronica Grondona (eds.), *The Indigenous Languages of South America: A Comprehensive Guide*, Mouton de Gruyter, 2012, pp. 167–234\n- *El Ojo verde — Cosmovisiones amazónicas*; Parks Watch, Reserva Comunal Asháninka de Satipo\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ash%C3%A1ninka\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ash%C3%A1ninka_language\n- British Museum online collection: https://www.britishmuseum.org/collection\n- Smithsonian Folkways: https://folkways.si.edu/search?query=Ashaninka\n\n",
